  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Scholarly notes discuss proposed interpretations of the name Gilgamesh, including an association with the word for axe and a possible meaning related to offspring. The notes also describe Enkidu as born in the field, compare two accounts of creation involving Aruru, the man of Anu, and the offspring of Ninib, and draw a cautious analogy between the woman who guides Enkidu and the woman/Eve terminology in Genesis.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The notes state that an artificial interpretation seeks to understand the first syllable of Gilgamesh as meaning \"hero.\"",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "A proposed West Semitic form, Bilga-Mash, is interpreted as possibly meaning \"the offspring of Mash.\"",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The axe is described as a prominent instrument in the Epic and as an object wielded by Gilgamesh.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Enkidu is repeatedly described as being born in the field.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "The notes describe the man of Anu as created by Aruru's heart, understood as her will, while the offspring of Ninib is described as moulded from clay.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "A woman who guides Enkidu is identified by the Babylonian term harimtu, meaning \"woman.\"",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The notes state that Eve's name appears only after the fall in Genesis, while the Babylonian woman is referred to as woman before that point in the comparison.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "The notes explicitly state that an analogy between tales does not establish dependence and that similar traditions may have arisen independently.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    }
