  },
  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "the companions are also twins or at least brothers",
    "summary": "The excerpt consists of scholarly notes on the Gilgamesh tradition. It discusses Enkidu's association with a woman and with preexisting human society, interprets the Deluge and primeval waters as seasonal nature-myth material, identifies a Deluge survivor, and discusses the twin-brother relationship of Gilgamesh and Enkidu alongside comparative examples.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "quoted"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The union of Enkidu with a woman is described as the beginning of a higher life, while the hunter and his father indicate that human society already existed.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The note characterizes the combination of Enkidu's primitive-man elements with the hunter, his father, and the woman as containing inconsistent features typical of folk-tales.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The Deluge is described as an annual overflow of the Euphrates Valley rivers and as marking a change from the dry season to the rainy season.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The canal system is said to transform the river overflow from a curse into a blessing.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "The note contrasts the Deluge with a creation account in which primeval waters dry up and the earth appears during the transition from the rainy season to the dry season.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "The month called the Mission of Ishtar alludes to Ishtar's disappearance from earth and her mission to the lower world.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The Sumerian survivor's name is interpreted as referring to lengthened or long life, while Ut-napishtim is presented as an abbreviated Akkadian rendering.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "The commentary states that Gilgamesh and Enkidu's companions are twins or at least brothers, and distinguishes them from pairs who are companions without being regarded as brothers.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "The primeval pair Yama-Yami and Iranian equivalents are mentioned as possible related examples.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "The woman tells Enkidu that Gilgamesh already knows of his coming through dreams interpreted by Ninsun; Gilgamesh then sets out and encounters Enkidu.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    }
  ],
