  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "Loki turned himself into a mare ... Loki gave birth to Othins eight-legged horse, Sleipnir.",
    "summary": "The notes discuss Loki's transformation into a mare, his resulting offspring Sleipnir, genealogical and mythological relationships among Norse figures, Fenrir's binding and eschatological role, the fire-world host led by Surt, and variant accounts of Loki's punishment.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "quoted"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The note states that there is no other reference to Loki having spent eight years underground or to his cow-milking.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "A giant undertook to build a fortress for the gods in exchange for Freyja, the sun, and the moon if the work was completed by a specified time.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The giant's sole helper was his horse, Svathilfari.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "When the fortress was nearly complete and the gods feared losing the promised rewards, Loki changed into a mare and distracted Svathilfari from his task.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Shortly afterward, Loki gave birth to Othin's eight-legged horse, Sleipnir.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Othin is identified as a god of magic, and the note says there is no other reference to him disguising himself as a witch.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Frigg is identified as Othin's wife.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Vili and Ve are identified as Othin's brothers and as sons of Bur and Bestla.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Baldr was killed through Loki's cunning by the blind Hoth.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Freyja is identified as the daughter of Njorth and the sister of Freyr.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Njorth is identified as the father of Freyr and Freyja and as a hostage given by the Wanes in exchange for Hnir at the end of the first war.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "The note reports that the Ynglingasaga supports a story in which Njorth had two children by his sister before joining the gods, while Snorri states that Freyr and Freyja were born after Njorth came to the gods.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Tyr is identified as a god of battle, and Fenrir is identified as Loki's wolf son who awaits in chains until the final battle.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "According to the note, Fenrir's slaver runs from his mouth and forms the river called Vam while he remains chained until the doom of the gods.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "Gerth is identified as the daughter of Gymir, and the notes associate Freyr's loss of his sword with the Skirnismol.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:12"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "Muspells sons are described as inhabitants of the fire-world, led by Surt against the gods in the last battle; Myrkwood is described here as the forest boundary of that fire-world.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:12"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:17",
      "text": "Heimdall is described as the watchman of the gods and as a god of light; the note says that he and Loki kill one another in the last battle.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:18",
      "text": "One account says that the gods bound Loki with the bowels of his son Vali and changed his other son, Narfi, into a wolf; Snorri's account reverses these roles.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:14"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:19",
      "text": "Narfi and Vali are identified as sons of Loki and his wife Sigyn.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:15"
      ]
    }
