    "quote": "At last were born   |   a boy and girl,",
    "summary": "The introductory note discusses the poem's manuscript position, dating, provenance, and relation to German versions of the Sigurth-Atli legend. The prose frame identifies Borgny as Heithrek's daughter and Vilmund as her lover. In the poem, Oddrun travels by horse to Borgny, who is in severe difficulty giving birth. Oddrun chants magic charms, after which a boy and a girl are born. The women then discuss an old oath, past grievances, Gunnar, Oddrun's former happiness in a hero's hall, her father's marriage instructions, and Brynhild's story.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "quoted"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The introductory note states that Oddrun is Atlis sister, is absent from extant German versions, and is generally assumed to be a northern addition to the legend.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Heithrek is identified as a king, Borgny as his daughter, and Vilmund as Borgny's lover; Borgny cannot give birth until Oddrun comes to her.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Oddrun brings a bit-bearer and a black horse from its stall, saddles the horse, rides across level ground, reaches a tall hall, and removes the saddle.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "A serving-maid tells Oddrun that Borgny lies in bitter pain and would find help from her.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "The serving-maid identifies Vilmund as Borgny's lover and says that he had wrapped Borgny in warm bedclothes while her father remained unaware for five winters.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Oddrun sits at Borgny's knees and chants potent charms for her with magic and force.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "A boy and a girl are born, described as the son and daughter of Hognis slayer; Borgny then becomes able to speak and thanks Oddrun.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Borgny invokes Frigg, Freyja, and favoring gods in wishing help and favor for Oddrun.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Oddrun says that she came to help because she had fulfilled an old oath to aid all who shared the wealth of warriors.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Borgny says that she followed Oddrun wherever Oddrun went, as though they had been born of two brothers.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Borgny recalls that she once gave Gunnar a draught and that Oddrun said no maiden had performed such a deed except herself.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "Oddrun says that she had been happy in a hero's hall, loved by the warriors, and glad of her father's gifts for five winters while her father lived.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Before his death, Heithrek ordered that Oddrun receive a dower of red gold and marry Grimhild's son.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:12"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "Heithrek ordered Brynhild to wear a helm and said that she should be a bright wish-maid and an exceptionally noble woman.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "Brynhild is described as possessing lands and folk; earth and heaven resounded when Fafnirs slayer saw the city, and a battle with foreign swords later broke the city.",
      "category": "sequence",
