    "summary": "Footnotes 33-51 explain names, military terms, character attributes, beliefs about destiny, literary parallels, medicinal restoratives, heroic traditions, and differing accounts of Alexander's death.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The footnote states that Gurz can signify a wild boar, although orthodox Muslim interpretation does not accept that meaning in the text; it also says that the warrior's name corresponds to the bearings on the standard.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The commentary describes family bearings as a widespread practice and reports that Japanese people wore family arms on their clothing, with a married woman adopting her husband's arms.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The commentary attributes Sohrb's failure to boast of his father's fame, and his questioning of Rustem as though selecting him for destruction, to destiny and Persian fatalism.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "A speech is compared to a piece of tapestry whose figures are visible when spread open and hidden when folded.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "The commentary recounts that Themistocles requested time from Xerxes and used that time to learn Persian well enough to speak with the king without an interpreter.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Hujr is identified as the son of Gdarz, and the footnote comments that large royal families were not unusual in the East.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The Kulub-gah is defined as the center or heart of an army where the sovereign or chief usually remains.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Ahirmun is identified as a demon and as the principle of evil.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "A girdle is described as a gift from a king serving as a token of affection and gratitude.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "In Firdusi's war imagery, a crocodile is described as a figure of great power and strength.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "The commentary states that the famous heroes in the Shahnama are distinguished both by address and cunning and by bravery.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "Umud is identified as an iron weapon and is also said to signify a column or beam.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "A maxim attributed to Sa'di warns against calculating on the weakness or insignificance of an enemy.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "Rustem is described as distinguished for piety as well as bravery, and as attributing success, power, and victory to Heaven or God.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:12"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "A passage is described in which a person who has thirsted for blood is told that Destiny will thirst for his blood and that the hairs on his body will become daggers against him.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "Jemshid's glory and misfortunes are described as recurring subjects of admiration and reflection among Persian poets.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:14"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:17",
      "text": "Medicated draughts are said to occur frequently in romances, including accounts of drugs intended to restore the dead in Indian medical books.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:15"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:18",
      "text": "Zra is said to have conducted Afrasiyab's troops across the Jihun while Rustem remained on the battlefield.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:16"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:19",
      "text": "Manjeh is identified as the daughter of Afrasiyab.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:17"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:20",
      "text": "The commentary mentions a Greek singing-girl at a festival of Adonis and female musicians surrounding the Caliph at feasts in the Arabian Nights.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:18"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:21",
      "text": "Kashan is presented in the text as Alexander's deathplace, whereas Greek historians are said to place his sudden death at Babylon.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:19"
      ]
    }
  ],
