      "text": "Saf was entrusted with guarding an imperial signet ring possessing magical properties, and the ring was stolen while in his care.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "When Solomon granted an audience to animals and insects, an ant brought a blade of grass as an offering and rebuked Saf for careless guardianship.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Majnun is identified as a celebrated lover who was maddened by Lail's charms.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The world is described metaphorically as a shrine consumed by topers, while earlier revellers' heads are described as potter's clay in the earth.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "The wild tulip of Shiraz has a dark central mark that is compared with a preordained brand of love on Hafez's heart.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Khosru is explained as a title used for several ancient Persian kings and, in the passage, as a plural term for monarchs generally.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Korah is described as a miser who disobeyed Moses, was swallowed by the earth with his treasures, and is said still to be sinking deeper.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Solomon's minister Saf, his east-wind courser, and his knowledge of the language of birds are presented as glories erased by time.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Mount Kaf is described as a fabulous mountain encircling the world.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Aiman is identified as the valley where God appeared to Moses and is used metaphorically for the abode of the Beloved.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Kalandars are described as wandering and begging Muslim dervishes; the footnote distinguishes worthless sectarians from a true spiritual Kalandar who follows a path shaped by duty.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "Zoroaster is identified as a prophet of fire-worshippers, and Nimrod is said to have practiced a similar religion.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Ad and Thamud are identified as Arab tribes exterminated by God for disobeying the prophet Salih.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "A grain means a grain of wheat, identified in Muslim tradition as the forbidden fruit of Paradise.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "Kamil is identified as an Arab whose glance inflicted death.",
